fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let A \in  \mathbb {C}^{m\times  n} . A partitioning of A is a representation of A in the form

    A = \begin {bmatrix}
        A_{11} & A_{12} & \cdots  & A_{1q} \\
        A_{21} & A_{22} & \cdots  & A_{2q} \\
        \vdots  & \vdots  & \ddots  & \vdots  \\
        A_{p1} & A_{p2} & \cdots  & A_{pq}
    \end {bmatrix}

where A_{ij} \in  \mathbb {C}^{m_i \times  n_j}  for 1 \leq  i \leq  p and 1 \leq  j \leq  q such that

    \sum _{i=1}^p m_i = m \quad  \text {and} \quad  \sum _{j=1}^q n_j = n.

The partitioned matrix operations are similar to the operations on the normal matrix.

If the partitioned matrix is formed as diagonal blocks, then we can compute the determinant of the matrix by the following formula:

    \det  A = \det  A_1 \cdot  \det  A_2 \cdots  \det  A_n

And the inverse of the matrix is

    A^{-1} = \begin {bmatrix}
        A_1^{-1} & O & \cdots  & O \\
        O & A_2^{-1} & \cdots  & O \\
        \vdots  & \vdots  & \ddots  & \vdots  \\
        O & O & \cdots  & A_n^{-1}
    \end {bmatrix}

The column partitioning of matrix is useful. If we have m\times s matrix A = (a_{ij}) and s\times n matrix B=(b_{ij}), their product can be written:
AB = \begin {bmatrix} A_1 \\ A_2 \\ \vdots A_m \end {bmatrix} \begin {bmatrix} B_1 & B_2 & \cdots & B_n \end {bmatrix} = \begin {bmatrix} A_1B_1 & A_1B_2 & \cdots & A_1B_n \\ A_2B_1 & A_2B_2 & \cdots & A_2B_n \\ \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\ A_mB_1 & A_mB_2 & \cdots & A_mB_n \end {bmatrix}
We can show that A=O\iff A^TA=O.

For instance, matrix A = \begin {bmatrix}1&2&1\\-2&-3&1\\3&5&0\end {bmatrix} can be transformed into reduced row-echelon form:
\begin {aligned}{\begin {bmatrix}1&2&1\\-2&-3&1\\3&5&0\end {bmatrix}}&\xrightarrow  {2R_{1}+R_{2}\to  R_{2}} {\begin {bmatrix}1&2&1\\0&1&3\\3&5&0\end {bmatrix}}\xrightarrow  {-3R_{1}+R_{3}\to  R_{3}} {\begin {bmatrix}1&2&1\\0&1&3\\0&-1&-3\end {bmatrix}}\\&\xrightarrow  {R_{2}+R_{3}\to  R_{3}} \,\,{\begin {bmatrix}1&2&1\\0&1&3\\0&0&0\end {bmatrix}}\xrightarrow  {-2R_{2}+R_{1}\to  R_{1}} {\begin {bmatrix}1&0&-5\\0&1&3\\0&0&0\end {bmatrix}}~.\end {aligned}
The final matrix has two non-zero rows and thus the rank of matrix A is 2.

Let V be a vector space over a field F. Let v_1, \dots , v_n be vectors in V. The span of v_1, \dots , v_n is defined as
\text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_n) = \{a_1 v_1 + \dots + a_n v_n \mid a_i \in F\}
The span of empty set is defined to be \{0\}.

The light-cone coordinates can be defined as two independent linear combinations of the time and a chosen spatial coordinate (conventionally x^1):
\begin {align*} x^+ \equiv \frac {1}{\sqrt {2}} (X^0 + X^1) \\ x^- \equiv \frac {1}{\sqrt {2}} (X^0 - X^1) \end {align*}
while other spatial coordinates remain unchanged. Thus the complete set of light-cone coordinates is (x^+,x^-,x^2,x^3).
